<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zafadola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1146</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad Sayf al-Dawla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zafadola, as Christians called Ahmad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayf-al-dawla">Sayf al-Dawla</a>, was the last prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Hudid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Holding the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rueda-de-jalon">Rueda de Jalón</a>, he became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a>, exchanging Rueda for lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, and tried to win power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> as the Almoravid regime collapsed. He was killed in battle near Chinchilla in 1146.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zafadola.jpg" alt="Zafadola" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ecelan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rueda_de_Jal%C3%B3n_-_Castillo_-_Muralla_neoarabe.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
